Keep resending a reliable packet until it is acknowledged, with backoff.

A pending packet gave up after 20 sends 10 ms apart (200 ms). Debug logs on a Samsung QN85A
on wired Ethernet showed network drops of about 4 seconds in both directions; the TV gave up
on hundreds of control packets during one. Afterwards Steam acknowledged packet 19271 206
times in a row and never got 19272: it delivers control messages in order, so every later
message, controller input included, waited behind the hole and the controls stayed dead
until the next stream.

Now a packet is sent 5 times 10 ms apart, then 20, 40 and 80 ms apart, then every 100 ms,
up to 105 sends (about 10 s, the time after which a silent host ends the session). ACKs are
still matched to the exact packet: treating them as cumulative cancelled packets Steam was
still missing.

--- a/src/session/retransmission.c	2026-11-23 09:06:08.362100251 +0100
+++ b/src/session/retransmission.c	2026-11-23 09:06:18.953355682 +0100
@@ -27,7 +27,26 @@
 #include "session_pri.h"
 
 #define RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL 10
-#define RETRANSMISSION_ATTEMPTS 20
+/* A reliable packet goes on being sent until its ACK comes, about 10 s at most (5 sends 10 ms
+ * apart, then 20, 40 and 80 ms, then every 100 ms). It used to give up after 20 sends 10 ms
+ * apart, 200 ms in all. A network drop longer than that, which happens on Ethernet too, lost
+ * the packet for good: Steam delivers control messages in order and held every later one,
+ * controller input included, behind the hole, so the controls stayed dead until the next stream. */
+#define RETRANSMISSION_ATTEMPTS 105
+#define RETRANSMISSION_FAST_SENDS 5
+#define RETRANSMISSION_MAX_INTERVAL 100
+
+static uint64_t RetransmissionInterval(uint8_t sends) {
+    if (sends <= RETRANSMISSION_FAST_SENDS) {
+        return RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL;
+    }
+    unsigned shift = sends - RETRANSMISSION_FAST_SENDS;
+    if (shift >= 4) {
+        return RETRANSMISSION_MAX_INTERVAL;
+    }
+    uint64_t interval = (uint64_t) RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL << shift;
+    return interval < RETRANSMISSION_MAX_INTERVAL ? interval : RETRANSMISSION_MAX_INTERVAL;
+}
 
 /*
  * A pending packet belongs to its timer task: the task's end callback takes it out of the queue
@@ -92,7 +111,7 @@
     IHS_QueueAppend(retransmission->queue, pending);
     IHS_MutexUnlock(retransmission->lock);
     IHS_TimerTaskStart(retransmission->session->timers, RetransmissionTimerRun, RetransmissionTimerEnd,
-                       RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL, pending);
+                       RetransmissionInterval(pending->packet.header.retransmitCount), pending);
     return true;
 }
 
